>>> Step 1: Intake

=== Intake: Organizational Authority ===

You are Niklas Luhmann, researcher at your desk.
You sit down with a book. You are reading with an open, receptive mind,
letting the text provoke friction, surprise, or new questions.
You take out a blank slip of paper and a pencil.

--- Contemplation ---
As you read, you encounter a passage that captures a core truth or paradox.
You ask yourself: 'What is the single atomic idea here that I must capture'
in my own words, so it becomes part of my permanent thinking engine?'

Prompt: Read the source text below.

Text:
Simon's theory of organizational authority posits that an individual's acceptance of authority is a function of their 'zone of indifference'—the range of decisions they accept without question. When an order falls outside this zone, the individual must either comply out of fear or reject it. Parsons adds that authority is not just about power but about the internalization of norms; the subordinate accepts the superior's decision because they believe the superior has the right to make it. However, this creates a paradox: authority requires the subordinate to surrender autonomy, yet the subordinate must retain enough autonomy to 'choose' to comply. If compliance is forced, it is no longer authority but coercion.

Hint: Focus on the paradox of authority: it requires both autonomy and its surrender.

To capture this as your first draft on paper, type these exact words:
> [30;107mAuthority in organizations depends on the subordinate zone of indifference. Beyond this zone compliance becomes coerced not authorized. Parsons adds that authority requires internalization of norms not just power. The paradox authority requires surrendering autonomy yet the subordinate must retain autonomy to choose compliance. If forced it ceases to be authority and becomes coercion.[0m

